# Coil — Debug and automation API

Coil ships a small debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven and
inspected from code, without touching the keyboard or waiting on real time. It is
what you use to iterate on the simulation, reproduce a specific run, write
automated checks of the mechanics, and capture clean screenshots of an exact game
state. This file defines that surface. Implement all of it, on the same footing as
the game itself.

Nothing here changes how a person plays. The debug API is inert during normal
play, doing nothing until something calls it, and the debug overlay is off until
toggled.

## A deterministic core

The whole surface rests on the simulation being deterministic and steppable, which
the game loop in `specs/movement.md` already requires: a fixed 125 ms tick — 8
ticks per second, exactly, for the whole round — advanced in whole ticks and
decoupled from rendering. That fixed rate is what makes a tick a unit: it is the
unit this API measures time in. Two more properties make it driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances by ticking the simulation and must not
  depend on a canvas, on `requestAnimationFrame`, or on wall-clock time to make
  progress. Rendering reads the state, never the other way around.
- Seeded randomness. The pellet placement (`specs/board.md`) runs off a seedable
  generator, so reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same pellet
  sequence exactly.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of API calls and steps, the game reaches
the same state every time.

## The `window.__coil` object

Expose the API as a single object on the global `window.__coil`, installed once the
game is running. It carries a `version` number (use `1`) and the operations below.
Values are plain numbers, strings, booleans, and `{col, row}` cells so a caller can
read them directly; cell coordinates are the integer grid indices of
`specs/board.md`.

### Core operations

- `reset(options)` returns the game to its initial title state. `options` is
  optional, and `options.seed` (a number) seeds the pellet-placement randomness so
  a run replays identically. After `reset`, the keyboard resumes control of the
  game until a control operation below takes over.
- `step(ticks)` advances the simulation by exactly `ticks` fixed ticks
  immediately, running the real 125 ms tick internally rather than waiting for real
  frames. The unit is ticks, not seconds: `step(8)` advances one second of game
  time, `step(1)` advances one tick. It runs exactly the number of ticks asked for,
  with no rounding, no accumulator, and no partial tick — `step(3)` runs three
  ticks and stops. A `ticks` that is not a non-negative whole number (a fraction, a
  negative, a non-number) is invalid: reject it with a clear error rather than
  rounding or clamping it, so a caller can never silently measure a different
  number of ticks than it asked for. `step(0)` is valid and does nothing. This runs
  the real simulation forward from a set-up state to see where it lands. Stepping
  only advances a live round (the `playing` state); on a menu or paused screen, and
  once a round has ended, it does nothing.
- `snapshot()` returns a plain, JSON-serializable object describing the current
  game state (see [Snapshot shape](#snapshot-shape)). It is a pure read and never
  changes anything.

### The manual clock

Normal play advances the simulation from the wall clock: the animation-frame loop
feeds each frame's elapsed time to the fixed-step update, so the snake steps 8
times a second on its own. That is the `autoStep` mode, and it is on by default.

`reset()` and `step()` switch the game to manual stepping: they set `autoStep` to
false, and while it is false the animation-frame loop still renders every frame but
does not advance the simulation. `step(ticks)` is then the only thing that
advances it, so a scripted scenario runs exactly the ticks it asks for and is
reproducible regardless of machine load; no stray wall-clock frame can slip an
extra tick into a measurement.

- `setAutoStep(enabled)` chooses the clock. `setAutoStep(true)` lets the game
  advance itself in real time again, which is how you watch or record a live motion
  clip; `setAutoStep(false)` returns to manual stepping. `reset()` re-arms manual
  stepping. The control and input operations below do not change `autoStep` on
  their own.

Measure in the default manual mode and read exact values back; switch to
`setAutoStep(true)` only when you want the game to play on in real time.

### Control operations

These set up a specific situation. Each one routes through the same systems normal
play uses, arranging the board rather than faking outcomes. They do not advance the
simulation; a following `step()` runs the real tick forward from what they set up.

- `startRound()` begins a round in the game's mode, exactly as choosing the play
  entry from the title menu would. The game enters the live `playing` state with
  the snake at its start position (`specs/board.md`) and the first pellet placed.
- `setSnake(cells, dir)` poses the snake as the chain `cells`, an array of
  `{col, row}` from the head first to the tail last, with `dir` its current facing
  (`"up"`, `"down"`, `"left"`, or `"right"`). It routes through the real snake
  model, so the next tick advances and resolves collision from exactly this
  configuration, and it clears any buffered turn. Use it to arrange a precise
  movement, turning, or collision scenario.
- `setPellet(cell)` places the current pellet at `cell` (`{col, row}`). The next
  tick eats it normally if the head advances into that cell. Use it to force a
  specific eat, or to set the last free cell of a board-filling scenario.
- `setCombo(multiplier, windowSeconds)` sets the combo multiplier and the seconds
  remaining on its window as a precondition; the next eat or the window's lapse
  then resolves through the real tick (`specs/combo.md`). A `windowSeconds` of `0`
  leaves the window closed.
- `setScore(points)` sets the current score directly, as a precondition. The high
  score still resolves through real play, so drive a real eat to push `BEST` past
  it.

A typical check calls `startRound()`, then `setSnake` and `setPellet` to arrange
the exact situation wanted, `step()` a tick or two to run the real ticks, and reads
the result from `snapshot()`.

### Input operations

The control operations above pose the board directly. The API can also inject
keyboard input, so a caller can drive the game the way a player does: navigate the
menus, start a round from the title, steer the snake, pause, and toggle mute.
Injected input flows through the same handling the real keyboard feeds, exercising
the actual key bindings from `specs/interface.md` rather than a parallel path. This
is how a caller confirms the controls themselves work.

- `keyDown(code)` presses a key down. `code` is a standard `KeyboardEvent.code`
  (for example `"ArrowUp"`, `"ArrowDown"`, `"ArrowLeft"`, `"ArrowRight"`,
  `"KeyW"`, `"KeyA"`, `"KeyS"`, `"KeyD"`, `"Enter"`, `"Space"`, `"Escape"`,
  `"KeyP"`, `"KeyM"`). Any one-shot action the key triggers on the current screen
  (a menu move, a confirm, a steering request buffered for the next tick, a pause,
  a mute toggle) is applied immediately.
- `keyUp(code)` releases a previously pressed key.
- `press(code)` is a convenience tap, a `keyDown` immediately followed by `keyUp`.
  This is the usual way to trigger a one-shot action (moving a menu selection,
  confirming it, requesting a turn, pausing, muting).

The usual shape for an input-driven scenario is to `press` through the menu to
start a round, then `press` a steering key and `step` one or more ticks so the turn
takes effect, reading `snapshot()` to see where the snake went.

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot()` returns an object with at least these fields:

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "paused" | "gameover" | "cleared",
  mode: {{#if (eq variant.slug "maze")}}"maze"{{else}}"classic"{{/if}},
  score: <number>,
  best: <number>,                 // the persisted high score (BEST)
  combo: <number>,                // the multiplier M, in [1, 5]
  comboWindow: <number>,          // seconds of sim-time left on the combo window; 0 = closed
  comboFraction: <number>,        // the window as a 0..1 fraction of full (the draining HUD bar)
  muted: <boolean>,               // whether the mute toggle is currently on
  ticks: <number>,                // ticks elapsed in the current round
  simTime: <number>,              // accumulated simulation time this round, in seconds
  dir: "up" | "down" | "left" | "right",   // the snake's current facing
  snake: [ { col: <number>, row: <number> }, ... ],   // segments, head first to tail last
  length: <number>,               // the snake's length in cells
  pellet: { col: <number>, row: <number> } | null,    // null once the board is cleared
{{#if (eq variant.slug "maze")}}
  // The fixed course of fatal interior obstacle cells (specs/mode.md).
  obstacles: [ { col: <number>, row: <number> }, ... ],
{{else}}
  // No interior obstacles in this mode, so this is always empty.
  obstacles: [],
{{/if}}
  menuIndex: <number>,            // the highlighted entry on a menu-bearing screen
  ended: <boolean>,               // whether the round has ended
  endReason: "dead" | "cleared" | null,
}
```

`snake` lists the segments in order from the head (index `0`) to the tail, each an
integer `{col, row}` cell. `comboWindow` counts down in simulation seconds and is
`0` when the window is closed, so a caller that eats a pellet can watch it reopen
and drain. `pellet` is the single live pellet, or `null` once the board is cleared.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "maze")}}
`obstacles` is the mode's fixed obstacle course, the same cells that are fatal on
contact and excluded from pellet placement (specs/mode.md).
{{/if}}

## The debug overlay

Provide a read-only on-screen overlay showing the game's live internal state, so
you can watch what the simulation is doing while you play. It is toggled with the
backtick key (`` ` ``), off by default, and never changes gameplay; it only draws.

When on, it draws over the running game, legibly, in the game's monospace type, at
least: the current `screen` and `mode`, the score and `BEST`, the combo multiplier
and the seconds left on its window, the snake's facing, length, and head cell, and
the pellet cell, the same facts `snapshot()` reports. It is a diagnostic layer
rather than part of the game's presentation, so keep it visually plain and clearly
separate from the HUD.
